    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d27">

                <pb facs="0019" n="13" sameAs="frus:frus1939v05/pg13"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.24/42: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Venezuela</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Scott</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-07-10T21:00:00-04:00">July 10, 1939—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>50. Your no. 57, June 28, 5 p.m. No formal statement is required for the purposes
                    outlined in the Department’s circular telegram of June 27 but merely an informal
                    indication of the attitude of the Venezuelan Government. Nearly all replies have
                    been received and all have been favorable.</p>
                <p>Since you will already have received the full text of the resolution, it is hoped
                    that the favorable response of the Venezuelan Government will be forthcoming.
                    You may emphasize of course that Dr. Gil Borges’<anchor n="10" xml:id="d27-f1"/>
                    views would be regarded as entirely confidential.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d27-f1" n="10" xml:id="d27-f1-content">
                <p>Venezuelan Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
